{:edb/db-before-t 1268 :edb/db-after-t 1269 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534581 50 #inst "2026-10-12T03:42:03.801Z" 13194139534581 17592186045417 true] [17592186092074 1071 "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n![](images/36/36c1ce5342657231cd78154ab4821a6a5bf9b78b74297ae5a4cdccdc2f6d5dcd.svg)" 13194139534581 17592186045417 true] [17592186092074 1071 "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n![](images/90/901d3a22065b48a8076b9c9aae5563c770c36c9ad40e4063c0f739847e1cda98.png)" 13194139534581 17592186045417 false] [17592186092074 1074 "First, we find the matrix $\\, A-\\lambda I\\mathbin{:}$\n﻿﻿﻿![](images/0c/0c6f9f860a1cf4d510f6c3d4d9c66b77496a354bb1e2cef1b69fd1178e4f15ab.png)\n\nThen, the characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 3 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-4\\cdot 3 & =0 \\\\ 4-4\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}-12 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -8 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534581 17592186045417 false] [17592186092074 1074 "First, we find the matrix $\\, A-\\lambda I\\mathbin{:}$\n﻿﻿﻿![](images/1c/1cab5be2ed7c560a3a5192473be4ff7b6cf6dc5728552f1d179c73affebefe20.svg)\n\nThen, the characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 3 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-4\\cdot 3 & =0 \\\\ 4-4\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}-12 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -8 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534581 17592186045417 true] [17592186092075 1071 "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix\n![](images/2c/2c669dd6f7c5fa368c5ea28961d2ef57cac9deaf7ba453f75a441b05206f6276.svg)" 13194139534581 17592186045417 true] [17592186092075 1071 "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix\n![](images/5c/5ca3e07cfbe9e5d4956b1eab4393601f2a78b0b067d17312e71cbac993e28a86.png)" 13194139534581 17592186045417 false] [17592186092075 1074 "Let's start by writing the matrix $B-\\lambda I\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/e4/e4e91261ec7f4a9a48847005a57730afa25f5e69a1f3818ad583acaadae639f8.svg)\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $\\det(B-\\lambda I) = 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-0\\cdot 0 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -4)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $B$ has only one eigenvalue $\\lambda=4,$ which is a double root of the characteristic equation. \n\n**Note:** We also say that $\\lambda=4$ has an **algebraic multiplicity** of $2.$" 13194139534581 17592186045417 true] [17592186092075 1074 "Let's start by writing the matrix $B-\\lambda I\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/fd/fdbcc62870aefab6214b2f24f53e9bc5654e5386639d4bc328dff3769aff2b45.png)\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $\\det(B-\\lambda I) = 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-0\\cdot 0 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -4)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $B$ has only one eigenvalue $\\lambda=4,$ which is a double root of the characteristic equation. \n\n**Note:** We also say that $\\lambda=4$ has an **algebraic multiplicity** of $2.$" 13194139534581 17592186045417 false] [17592186092076 1071 "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix ![](images/9f/9f13c3379e0613ce91669f6e52473dd11dc95360c422943764f30220dbccf430.svg)" 13194139534581 17592186045417 true] [17592186092076 1071 "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix ![](images/a1/a1b88b7897649bb059cef39d40b2b052a13ac28282223b5688a1bce98296fb53.png)" 13194139534581 17592186045417 false] [17592186092076 1074 "Let's start by writing the matrix $A - \\lambda I\\mathbin{:}$\n﻿﻿![](images/03/039ee6073538580614cc07453132b811a4da81f438abf37197bb686057b4b66c.svg)\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $\\det(A-\\lambda I) = 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}10-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 6 & 7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (10-\\lambda )(7-\\lambda )-3\\cdot 6 & =0 \\\\ 70-7\\lambda -10\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}-18 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-17\\lambda +52 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{17\\pm \\sqrt{(-17)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 52}}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{17\\pm \\sqrt{81}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{17\\pm 9}{2} \\\\  & =13,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda=13$ and $\\lambda=4.$" 13194139534581 17592186045417 true] [17592186092076 1074 "Let's start by writing the matrix $A - \\lambda I\\mathbin{:}$\n﻿﻿![](images/ca/ca8f02e390e338b62956302ec45ea274ad73a51dafde3fe93b3d1e37dd663699.png)\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $\\det(A-\\lambda I) = 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}10-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 6 & 7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (10-\\lambda )(7-\\lambda )-3\\cdot 6 & =0 \\\\ 70-7\\lambda -10\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}-18 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-17\\lambda +52 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{17\\pm \\sqrt{(-17)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 52}}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{17\\pm \\sqrt{81}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{17\\pm 9}{2} \\\\  & =13,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda=13$ and $\\lambda=4.$" 13194139534581 17592186045417 false] [17592186092077 1071 "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix ![](images/60/603c59d43e3be000931e7279ac4d27be8aa92692f1528ec57ebd52172affa3cb.png)" 13194139534581 17592186045417 false] [17592186092077 1071 "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix ![](images/e3/e34b5bf690a1dd8f5275f2d742b264494386b95394bc7c32aaafb9eeba68e058.svg)" 13194139534581 17592186045417 true] [17592186092077 1074 "Let's start by writing the matrix $A - \\lambda I \\mathbin{:}$\n﻿﻿﻿![](images/37/37f547d6ad345415a1cc2480adf227ec29e31a351cf935e584b9544e44347310.svg)\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $\\det (A - \\lambda I) = 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 9 & -2\\sqrt{3}-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(-2\\sqrt{3}-\\lambda )-9\\cdot 0 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +2\\sqrt{3}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the eigenvalues $\\lambda={\\color{red}3}$ and $\\lambda={\\color{blue}-2\\sqrt{3}}.$\n\nIf we look again at the matrix ![](images/f4/f4e86a797cafe4857d5f135ec37d57e33368469767e50793f55b89ec7185c2eb.svg)\nwe see that the eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal. This is true in general: the eigenvalues of a *triangular* or *diagonal* matrix are *always* given by the entries on the main diagonal!\n\n**Watch out!** For a general matrix (which is neither triangular nor diagonal), this might not be the case!" 13194139534581 17592186045417 true] [17592186092077 1074 "Let's start by writing the matrix $A - \\lambda I \\mathbin{:}$\n﻿﻿﻿![](images/f9/f91e4886c58db10f60fa666a870aed3c2f456b25e6afb560f857e9c36e81217c.png)\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $\\det (A - \\lambda I) = 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 9 & -2\\sqrt{3}-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(-2\\sqrt{3}-\\lambda )-9\\cdot 0 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +2\\sqrt{3}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the eigenvalues $\\lambda={\\color{red}3}$ and $\\lambda={\\color{blue}-2\\sqrt{3}}.$\n\nIf we look again at the matrix ![](images/aa/aa89b93c6fabd2f3dc7063c85e253ded7482018a5f1c6dadedc74964194c5754.png)\nwe see that the eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal. This is true in general: the eigenvalues of a *triangular* or *diagonal* matrix are *always* given by the entries on the main diagonal!\n\n**Watch out!** For a general matrix (which is neither triangular nor diagonal), this might not be the case!" 13194139534581 17592186045417 false] [17592186092229 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426473 13194139534581 17592186045417 true] [17592186092229 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426474 13194139534581 17592186045417 true] [17592186092230 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426470 13194139534581 17592186045417 true] [17592186092232 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426471 13194139534581 17592186045417 true] [17592186092232 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426472 13194139534581 17592186045417 true] [17592186092464 1050 660.0 13194139534581 17592186045417 true] [17592186093438 1064 "Consider the matrix ![](images/63/63dd03e425762fa8e9e2e0d33ce552245c6d1c00a12022c433329c3c747cdbba.svg) How can we find its eigenvalues?\n\nBy definition, we need to find the value(s) of $\\,\\lambda\\,$ such that $\\,A\\mathbf{\\color{blue}v} = \\lambda \\mathbf{\\color{blue}v}\\,$ is true for some *non-zero* vector $\\mathbf{\\color{blue}v}.$ Let's re-write this equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ Av-\\lambda v & =0 \\\\ Av-\\lambda Iv & =0 \\\\ (A-\\lambda I)v & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation above tells us that there should be at least one non-zero vector $\\mathbf{\\color{blue}v}$ that lies in $\\textrm{Null}(A-\\lambda I)$, the null space of the matrix $A-\\lambda I.$ As a result, the invertible matrix theorem tells us that the matrix $\\, A-\\lambda I \\,$ must be singular, meaning $\\det(A-\\lambda I) = 0.$\n\nWe've reduced the problem to finding the values of $\\lambda$ that satisfy the equation\n\n$$\n\\det(A-\\lambda I) = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich is called the **characteristic equation** of the matrix $A$. So, let's solve it!\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix $A-\\lambda I\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\,-\\,\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\,-\\,\\lambda [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\,-\\,\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -1 & -4\\,-\\,\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve the characteristic equation $\\det(A-\\lambda I) = 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -1 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )-5\\cdot (-1) & =0 \\\\ -8-2\\lambda +4\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+5 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda=-3$ and $\\lambda=1.$" 13194139534581 17592186045417 true] [17592186093438 1064 "Consider the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{aligned}]$ How can we find its eigenvalues?\n\nBy definition, we need to find the value(s) of $\\,\\lambda\\,$ such that $\\,A\\mathbf{\\color{blue}v} = \\lambda \\mathbf{\\color{blue}v}\\,$ is true for some *non-zero* vector $\\mathbf{\\color{blue}v}.$ Let's re-write this equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ Av-\\lambda v & =0 \\\\ Av-\\lambda Iv & =0 \\\\ (A-\\lambda I)v & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation above tells us that there should be at least one non-zero vector $\\mathbf{\\color{blue}v}$ that lies in $\\textrm{Null}(A-\\lambda I)$, the null space of the matrix $A-\\lambda I.$ As a result, the invertible matrix theorem tells us that the matrix $\\, A-\\lambda I \\,$ must be singular, meaning $\\det(A-\\lambda I) = 0.$\n\nWe've reduced the problem to finding the values of $\\lambda$ that satisfy the equation\n\n$$\n\\det(A-\\lambda I) = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich is called the **characteristic equation** of the matrix $A$. So, let's solve it!\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix $A-\\lambda I\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\,-\\,\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\,-\\,\\lambda [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\,-\\,\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -1 & -4\\,-\\,\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve the characteristic equation $\\det(A-\\lambda I) = 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -1 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )-5\\cdot (-1) & =0 \\\\ -8-2\\lambda +4\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+5 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda=-3$ and $\\lambda=1.$" 13194139534581 17592186045417 false] [17592186426440 1080 "{\\lambda }^{2}+\\frac{1}{5}\\lambda =0" 13194139534581 17592186045417 true] [17592186426440 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534581 17592186045417 true] [17592186426440 1083 #uuid "0a127b5d-b607-5917-a20b-32fd0f904754" 13194139534581 17592186045417 true] [17592186426441 1080 "(\\sqrt{5}-\\lambda )(3+\\lambda )=0" 13194139534581 17592186045417 true] [17592186426441 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534581 17592186045417 true] [17592186426441 1083 #uuid "1579836b-928d-5630-a28f-d0c7c7253871" 13194139534581 17592186045417 true] [17592186426442 1080 "$\\lambda =5$ and $\\lambda =7$" 13194139534581 17592186045417 true] [17592186426442 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534581 17592186045417 true] [17592186426442 1083 #uuid "1ab8fa77-b07d-58b9-9474-d0c32cc4c09f" 13194139534581 17592186045417 true] [17592186426443 1080 "$\\lambda =5$ and $\\lambda =8$" 13194139534581 17592186045417 true] [17592186426443 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534581 17592186045417 true] [17592186426443 1083 #uuid "1fdedc73-108d-54bf-a80b-6f8d76425d70" 13194139534581 17592186045417 true] [17592186426444 1080 "There are no real eigenvalues" 13194139534581 17592186045417 true] [17592186426444 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534581 17592186045417 true] [17592186426444 1083 #uuid "21d98a0f-7b72-5084-9fcf-ee900921103a" 13194139534581 17592186045417 true] [17592186426445 1080 "$\\lambda =3$ and $\\lambda =1$" 13194139534581 17592186045417 true] [17592186426445 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 1319413953458